Rast wrote:
> Boudewijn Waijers wrote on Sat, 1 Jan 2005 11:20:21 +0100:

>> Note, however, that when a spellbook crumbles to dust as you read it,
>> it's usually because it's too high level for you, not because it is
>> cursed. So, writing a new one would only make sense when you wrote a
>> blessed one, since you'd probably be unable to read it *again*.

> The spellbook reading formula has a large random factor in it.

Agreed.

> Failure normally means you simply got a bad roll of the die and
> should try again (you've price-ID'd the book, right?).

I only price-id scrolls, and stop when I've identified identify. It
always feels a bit like cheating to me, although I cannot think of a
good way to eliminate it, short of randomly shuffeling prices at the
start of each game, like potion colours. Prices could be bound to scroll
NAMES instead of scroll types, for example, but I wouldn't advocate
that.

> A level 6 Monk with 10 INT has only a 75% chance of successfully
> reading a level 1 spellbook on the first try, but I wouldn't let that
> stop me from trying repeatedly.  The immediate benefit of a new spell
> (hopefully it isn't Knock) far outweighs the possible risk of
> destroying the book.

I rather keep the book until my level is higher, and won't risk
destroying the book, but that's a matter of playing style. Especially
with classes where both chance of reading and chance of casting are tied
to INT, you probably won't be able to cast the spell anyway, even if you
would bychance be able to study it.

> Waiting for holy water has its own set of risks.  Waiting until the
> chance is 100% is simply not reasonable -- our Monk needs 10 more
> levels or 5 more points of INT.

I'd settle for a 2/3 chance, also because the book is not always
destroyed when reading fails. It's not only destroying the book that I'd
rather not risk, though, it's also being teleported to another spot and
being frozen in place there. You never know what's lurking around there.

> In my experience it is easier to cast spells than to learn them.  If
> one has a robe, like the Monk in our example, most spells will be at
> 0% fail. Anyway, one doesn't need a 0% fail rate in a spell to make
> use of it, just as one doesn't need a 100% hit rate with a weapon to
> make use of it.

Not everyone is playing a monk, and robes are hard to come by for other
classes. Starting characters don't stand a chance at taking on a priest.

